solution_steps:
We know that $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} a_2b_3 - a_3b_2 \\ a_3b_1 - a_1b_3 \\ a_1b_2 - a_2b_1 \end{pmatrix}$,
where $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix}$.

In this case, we are given that $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 4 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix}$.

To find $\mathbf{a} \times (3 \mathbf{b})$, we can distribute the scalar 3 to each component of $\mathbf{b}$:

\begin{align*}
3 \mathbf{b} &= 3 \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 3b_1 \\ 3b_2 \\ 3b_3 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}

Now, we can find the cross product $\mathbf{a} \times (3 \mathbf{b})$ using the same formula as before:

\begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} \times (3 \mathbf{b}) &= \begin{pmatrix} a_2(3b_3) - a_3(3b_2) \\ a_3(3b_1) - a_1(3b_3) \\ a_1(3b_2) - a_2(3b_1) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 3(a_2b_3 - a_3b_2) \\ 3(a_3b_1 - a_1b_3) \\ 3(a_1b_2 - a_2b_1) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 3(5) \\ 3(4) \\ 3(-7) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 15 \\ 12 \\ -21 \end{pmatrix}.
